  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nThis strategy employs a multi-resolution hierarchical optimization approach combining global search with local refinement to escape local optima. Unlike previous local search algorithms, it starts from a coarse grid and progressively increases resolution while incorporating periodic restart mechanisms and dimensionality reduction. This prevents premature convergence by systematically introducing diversity through:\n\n- Variable projection and multi-resolution analysis\n- Hierarchical optimization across grid sizes (40\u2192400 points)\n- Population diversity maintenance through periodic restarts\n- Adaptive dimension sampling to focus computational resources\n- Layered local refinement using SLSQP, subgradient, and surgery methods\n\nThe primary bottleneck addressed is the curse of dimensionality in 400-variable space. Before execution, we validate the initial parent construction and project it to the coarsest grid for warm-starting global searches. Subsequently, by leveraging mixed-precision calculations and gradient-based polishing, we ensure both exploration and exploitation.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n- Variables: Piecewise-constant function h(x) represented by n-point samples, x\u2208[0,2], 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n- Objective: Minimize max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx = max_k (np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full') \u00b7 dx)\n- Representation: h as a real vector with \u2225h\u2225_\u221e\u22641 and \u2211h_i\u00b7dx=1\n- Operations:\n  - Grid transfer: H_coarse \u21a6 H_fine via `project_feasible`\n  - Multi-resolution projection: `interpolate_to(initial_h, n, target)`\n  - Objective: `compute_c5(h)`\n  - Constraints enforcement: `project_feasible(h, n/2)`\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\nPhase 0: Initialization\n- n_list = [40,80,160,400] grid points\n- \u03b4t = 20. s per resolution increase\n- Initialize DE projection parameter \u03b1=0.9, grad_tol=1e-4, perturb_scale=0.1\n\nFor each grid resolution n\u2208n_list:\n1. Project prior best (first grid uses parent if available)\n2. DE global phase:\n   - Initial population: 40 points (20 from random perturbations, 20 from H.coarse interpolation)\n   - Local steering: Enforced every 200 evals\n   - Fitness: `compute_c5(h)`\n\n3. Buried treasure refinement:\n   - Identify worst lags using `compute_corr`\n   - Apply targeted surgery: `worst_lag_surgery(h, max_steps=300)`\n\n4. Local polishing:\n   - Use `slsqp_round(h, n, [2000,8000], 4000)`\n   - Use `subgradient_polish(h, n, 500, 0.001, 0.995)`\n\n5. Dimension investment:\n   - Down-sample to n_fine=200 (from n=400)\n   - Enforce n_coarse=100 fallback if solution diverges\n\nFinal validation:\n- Double-check `sum(h)*dx \u2248 1`\n- Re-evaluate `compute_c5(h)`\n- Enforce final precision rounds\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n- Resolution: [40,80,160,400] with 10-second warmup per stage\n- Projection: \u03b4proj=0.9 (adjust to 0.7 if stuck)\n- Exploration ratio: startup 10-40% random, serene 1-10% random\n- Worst-case recovery: revert to n=40 solution if no progress for 4 hours\n- Bound tightening: enabled by `gradient_check_enabled = dtime < 700`\n- Local locking mechanism: max_iters=100 unless candidate improves\n\n5. Computational Budget and Robustness:\n- Primary: 800 s for 40\u2192160 grid increase\n- Secondary: 400 s for 160\u2192400 final optimization\n- Checkpointing: Save best candidate every 100 evaluations\n- Termination triggers:\n  - Time limit: budget_s - elapsed\n  - Early success: predefined checkpoints at 0.36-0.39\n  - Local detection: no improvement gradient checking\n- Fallback: Recover interpolation from nearest grid\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n1. Final candidate satisfaction:\n   - \u2211h[i]\u00b7dx \u2248 1 (log|(desired\u00b11e-7*|desired|, absolute target: |t_want-t_actual| < 1e-4)\n   - min(h[i])\u22650, max(h[i])\u22641 with tolerance 1e-5\n   - All correlation values finite (|c| > 0)\n2. Hierarchical compatibility:\n   - Project from domain to [0,1]^n (n=400)\n   - Compute final score via `compute_c5(h)`\n\nOutput format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) with n_points>400",
